{"subjectId":"c","id":"linked-list","title":"Singly Linked List","group":"Data Structures & Algorithms in C","order":41,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A chain of heap-allocated nodes, each pointing to the next. O(1) insertion at the head, O(n) search — and the most common C interview data structure.</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>head</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">[1 | •]</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">[2 | •]</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">[3 | •]</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>NULL</code></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct Node {\n    int data;\n    struct Node *next;\n} Node;\n\nNode *new_node(int v) {\n    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);\n    if (!n) { perror(\"malloc\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }\n    n-&gt;data = v;\n    n-&gt;next = NULL;\n    return n;\n}\n\nvoid push_front(Node **head, int v) {        /* O(1) */\n    Node *n = new_node(v);\n    n-&gt;next = *head;\n    *head = n;\n}\n\nvoid push_back(Node **head, int v) {         /* O(n): walk to the tail */\n    Node **pp = head;\n    while (*pp) pp = &amp;(*pp)-&gt;next;\n    *pp = new_node(v);\n}\n\nint remove_value(Node **head, int v) {       /* pointer-to-pointer: no special case for head */\n    for (Node **pp = head; *pp; pp = &amp;(*pp)-&gt;next) {\n        if ((*pp)-&gt;data == v) {\n            Node *dead = *pp;\n            *pp = dead-&gt;next;\n            free(dead);\n            return 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n\nvoid reverse(Node **head) {                  /* O(n), O(1) space */\n    Node *prev = NULL, *cur = *head;\n    while (cur) {\n        Node *next = cur-&gt;next;\n        cur-&gt;next = prev;\n        prev = cur;\n        cur = next;\n    }\n    *head = prev;\n}\n\nvoid print(const Node *n) {\n    for (; n; n = n-&gt;next) printf(\"%d -&gt; \", n-&gt;data);\n    printf(\"NULL\\n\");\n}\n\nvoid free_list(Node *n) {\n    while (n) { Node *next = n-&gt;next; free(n); n = next; }\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Node *head = NULL;\n    push_back(&amp;head, 2);\n    push_back(&amp;head, 3);\n    push_front(&amp;head, 1);\n    push_back(&amp;head, 4);\n    print(head);\n    remove_value(&amp;head, 1);\n    remove_value(&amp;head, 3);\n    print(head);\n    reverse(&amp;head);\n    print(head);\n    free_list(head);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; NULL\n2 -&gt; 4 -&gt; NULL\n4 -&gt; 2 -&gt; NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>Classic interview operations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct Node { int data; struct Node *next; } Node;\n\nNode *build(const int *a, int n) {\n    Node *head = NULL, **tail = &amp;head;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n        *tail = malloc(sizeof **tail);\n        if (!*tail) exit(1);\n        (*tail)-&gt;data = a[i];\n        (*tail)-&gt;next = NULL;\n        tail = &amp;(*tail)-&gt;next;\n    }\n    return head;\n}\n\nNode *middle(Node *head) {                   /* slow/fast pointers */\n    Node *slow = head, *fast = head;\n    while (fast &amp;&amp; fast-&gt;next) { slow = slow-&gt;next; fast = fast-&gt;next-&gt;next; }\n    return slow;\n}\n\nint has_cycle(const Node *head) {            /* Floyd's tortoise and hare */\n    const Node *slow = head, *fast = head;\n    while (fast &amp;&amp; fast-&gt;next) {\n        slow = slow-&gt;next;\n        fast = fast-&gt;next-&gt;next;\n        if (slow == fast) return 1;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n\nNode *merge_sorted(Node *a, Node *b) {       /* splice two sorted lists */\n    Node dummy = {0, NULL}, *t = &amp;dummy;\n    while (a &amp;&amp; b) {\n        if (a-&gt;data &lt;= b-&gt;data) { t-&gt;next = a; a = a-&gt;next; }\n        else                    { t-&gt;next = b; b = b-&gt;next; }\n        t = t-&gt;next;\n    }\n    t-&gt;next = a ? a : b;\n    return dummy.next;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x[] = {1, 4, 7, 9, 12};\n    int y[] = {2, 3, 10};\n    Node *a = build(x, 5), *b = build(y, 3);\n    printf(\"middle of a: %d\\n\", middle(a)-&gt;data);\n    printf(\"cycle? %d\\n\", has_cycle(a));\n\n    Node *m = merge_sorted(a, b);\n    for (Node *n = m; n; n = n-&gt;next) printf(\"%d \", n-&gt;data);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n\n    Node *last = m;                          /* make a cycle to test detection */\n    while (last-&gt;next) last = last-&gt;next;\n    last-&gt;next = m-&gt;next-&gt;next;\n    printf(\"cycle? %d\\n\", has_cycle(m));\n    last-&gt;next = NULL;                       /* break it again before freeing */\n\n    while (m) { Node *next = m-&gt;next; free(m); m = next; }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>middle of a: 7\ncycle? 0\n1 2 3 4 7 9 10 12\ncycle? 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Singly linked list</th><th>Array</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Access k-th</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Insert/delete at head</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Insert/delete after a known node</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Search</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n) (O(log n) if sorted)</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Extra pointer per node; scattered (cache-unfriendly)</td><td>Contiguous</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you reverse a singly linked list?</summary><div><p>Iteratively with three pointers: <code>prev = NULL</code>, <code>cur = head</code>; for each node save <code>next</code>, point <code>cur-&gt;next</code> to <code>prev</code>, then advance <code>prev = cur</code>, <code>cur = next</code>. At the end <code>prev</code> is the new head. O(n) time, O(1) space.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you detect a cycle in a linked list?</summary><div><p>Floyd’s algorithm: move a slow pointer one step and a fast pointer two steps; if they ever meet there is a cycle, if the fast pointer reaches <code>NULL</code> there isn’t. To find the cycle start, reset one pointer to the head and move both one step at a time — they meet at the entry.</p></div></details></section>"}